1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,449
A train has no steering wheel.

2
00:00:02,629 --> 00:00:05,092
Its wheels are fixed to a solid

3
00:00:05,092 --> 00:00:08,110
steel axle, so both wheels always turn

4
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:10,176
at exactly the same speed.

5
00:00:10,356 --> 00:00:12,422
And yet a train can go around

6
00:00:12,422 --> 00:00:15,130
a curve at three hundred kilometers an

7
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:17,624
hour without grinding itself apart.

8
00:00:17,804 --> 00:00:20,369
The secret is a shape most people

9
00:00:20,369 --> 00:00:21,379
never notice.

10
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:24,555
Train wheels are not flat cylinders.

11
00:00:24,735 --> 00:00:25,919
They are cones.

12
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:28,821
Today, ten parts of a railway,

13
00:00:28,821 --> 00:00:30,998
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:31,348 --> 00:00:34,363
Railways are almost two hundred years old,

15
00:00:34,363 --> 00:00:37,090
and most of their important parts were

16
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:39,529
invented in the eighteen hundreds,

17
00:00:39,529 --> 00:00:41,252
often after an accident.

18
00:00:41,432 --> 00:00:43,046
The wheels, the rails,

19
00:00:43,046 --> 00:00:45,688
the couplers and the brakes all look

20
00:00:45,688 --> 00:00:47,156
simple, even boring.

21
00:00:47,336 --> 00:00:50,070
But each one hides a clever solution

22
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:52,805
to a problem that once killed people

23
00:00:52,805 --> 00:00:54,325
or destroyed trains.

24
00:00:54,674 --> 00:00:57,545
As always, every fact comes from a

25
00:00:57,545 --> 00:01:01,345
named source, railway engineering references,

26
00:01:01,345 --> 00:01:05,145
accident investigations, and railway history,

27
00:01:05,145 --> 00:01:07,678
all listed in the description.

28
00:01:07,858 --> 00:01:09,763
Let's start with the shape of the

29
00:01:09,763 --> 00:01:10,110
wheel.

30
00:01:10,459 --> 00:01:13,617
Why are train wheels shaped like cones?

31
00:01:13,797 --> 00:01:16,792
Look closely at the surface that touches

32
00:01:16,792 --> 00:01:17,466
the rail.

33
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:18,842
It is not flat.

34
00:01:19,022 --> 00:01:20,798
It is slightly tapered,

35
00:01:20,798 --> 00:01:23,424
wider near the inside of the wheel

36
00:01:23,424 --> 00:01:25,895
and narrower toward the outside.

37
00:01:26,075 --> 00:01:28,003
When a train enters a curve,

38
00:01:28,003 --> 00:01:30,689
the wheelset shifts a little toward the

39
00:01:30,689 --> 00:01:32,135
outside of the curve.

40
00:01:32,315 --> 00:01:34,301
Now the outer wheel rides on its

41
00:01:34,301 --> 00:01:36,597
wider part, and the inner wheel rides

42
00:01:36,597 --> 00:01:37,900
on its narrower part.

43
00:01:38,249 --> 00:01:41,328
A wider circle covers more distance in

44
00:01:41,328 --> 00:01:42,058
one turn.

45
00:01:42,238 --> 00:01:45,224
So the outer wheel travels farther than

46
00:01:45,224 --> 00:01:46,449
the inner wheel,

47
00:01:46,449 --> 00:01:48,976
even though both turn at the same

48
00:01:48,976 --> 00:01:49,436
speed.

49
00:01:49,616 --> 00:01:51,923
That is exactly what you need in

50
00:01:51,923 --> 00:01:54,231
a curve, where the outer rail is

51
00:01:54,231 --> 00:01:54,736
longer.

52
00:01:54,916 --> 00:01:57,226
The wheelset steers itself.

53
00:01:57,406 --> 00:02:01,084
The physicist Richard Feynman loved this example,

54
00:02:01,084 --> 00:02:03,860
because it is pure geometry doing the

55
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:05,812
work of a steering system.

56
00:02:06,161 --> 00:02:07,641
On a straight track,

57
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:10,747
the cone also keeps the wheelset centered.

58
00:02:10,927 --> 00:02:12,593
If it drifts to one side,

59
00:02:12,593 --> 00:02:14,125
that wheel gets bigger,

60
00:02:14,125 --> 00:02:16,523
and it rolls back toward the middle.

61
00:02:16,703 --> 00:02:18,967
You can test this at home with

62
00:02:18,967 --> 00:02:21,835
two paper cups taped together at their

63
00:02:21,835 --> 00:02:22,590
wide ends.

64
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:25,398
Roll them on two parallel rulers,

65
00:02:25,398 --> 00:02:26,752
and they stay on.

66
00:02:26,932 --> 00:02:28,963
Tape them at the narrow ends,

67
00:02:28,963 --> 00:02:31,065
and they fall off immediately.

68
00:02:31,415 --> 00:02:34,014
Why does every train wheel have a

69
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:35,432
lip on the inside?

70
00:02:35,612 --> 00:02:37,277
It is called the flange,

71
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:39,497
and most people think it is what

72
00:02:39,497 --> 00:02:41,509
keeps the train on the rails.

73
00:02:41,689 --> 00:02:43,984
Actually, in normal running,

74
00:02:43,984 --> 00:02:45,869
the flange is a backup.

75
00:02:46,049 --> 00:02:48,178
The cone shape does most of the

76
00:02:48,178 --> 00:02:50,856
steering, and on a good straight track,

77
00:02:50,856 --> 00:02:53,259
the flange rarely touches the rail.

78
00:02:53,439 --> 00:02:55,484
It is there for sharp curves,

79
00:02:55,484 --> 00:02:58,233
switches, and the moments when the cone

80
00:02:58,233 --> 00:02:59,221
is not enough.

81
00:02:59,571 --> 00:03:02,021
When the flange does rub the rail,

82
00:03:02,021 --> 00:03:03,535
on very tight curves,

83
00:03:03,535 --> 00:03:04,400
you hear it.

84
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:07,194
That high pitched screech of a subway

85
00:03:07,194 --> 00:03:09,243
train in a curve is often the

86
00:03:09,243 --> 00:03:11,574
flange grinding against the rail,

87
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:14,400
or the wheels slipping because the solid

88
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:16,237
axle cannot adjust enough.

89
00:03:16,417 --> 00:03:19,445
Some railways put grease on the rails

90
00:03:19,445 --> 00:03:20,755
in tight curves,

91
00:03:20,755 --> 00:03:24,111
using machines called flange lubricators,

92
00:03:24,111 --> 00:03:26,157
to reduce wear and noise.

93
00:03:26,508 --> 00:03:28,906
The flange is always on the inside

94
00:03:28,906 --> 00:03:29,824
of the rails,

95
00:03:29,824 --> 00:03:31,093
never the outside.

96
00:03:31,273 --> 00:03:34,015
That way, the two flanges trap the

97
00:03:34,015 --> 00:03:35,547
rails between them,

98
00:03:35,547 --> 00:03:38,128
and a switch can guide the train

99
00:03:38,128 --> 00:03:40,143
by pushing on one flange.

100
00:03:40,323 --> 00:03:42,270
If you ever see a toy train

101
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:44,288
with flanges on the outside,

102
00:03:44,288 --> 00:03:46,883
it was designed by someone who never

103
00:03:46,883 --> 00:03:48,613
looked under a real one.

104
00:03:48,963 --> 00:03:51,297
Why are both wheels of a train

105
00:03:51,297 --> 00:03:53,165
fixed to one solid axle?

106
00:03:53,345 --> 00:03:55,305
A car has a differential,

107
00:03:55,305 --> 00:03:57,499
a set of gears that lets the

108
00:03:57,499 --> 00:04:00,242
outer wheel spin faster in a curve.

109
00:04:00,422 --> 00:04:02,354
Trains do not need one,

110
00:04:02,354 --> 00:04:05,798
because the cone shape already solves the

111
00:04:05,798 --> 00:04:06,470
problem.

112
00:04:06,650 --> 00:04:08,612
A solid axle is simple,

113
00:04:08,612 --> 00:04:11,426
very strong, and it keeps the two

114
00:04:11,426 --> 00:04:13,558
wheels perfectly aligned.

115
00:04:13,738 --> 00:04:17,334
On a machine that carries thousands of

116
00:04:17,334 --> 00:04:20,077
tons, simple and strong wins.

117
00:04:20,428 --> 00:04:22,552
But the cone has a side effect.

118
00:04:22,732 --> 00:04:23,801
At high speed,

119
00:04:23,801 --> 00:04:26,854
the self-centering motion can overshoot.

120
00:04:27,034 --> 00:04:29,674
The wheelset swings to one side,

121
00:04:29,674 --> 00:04:31,077
gets pushed back,

122
00:04:31,077 --> 00:04:33,470
overshoots to the other side,

123
00:04:33,470 --> 00:04:36,441
and starts swaying left and right in

124
00:04:36,441 --> 00:04:37,018
a wave.

125
00:04:37,198 --> 00:04:39,733
This is called hunting oscillation,

126
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:42,340
and it was described in the eighteen

127
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:42,992
eighties.

128
00:04:43,172 --> 00:04:44,272
At high speed,

129
00:04:44,272 --> 00:04:47,181
it can become violent and even damage

130
00:04:47,181 --> 00:04:47,967
the track.

131
00:04:48,317 --> 00:04:52,101
Engineers fight hunting with careful wheel shapes,

132
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:55,659
stiff connections between the wheelsets and the

133
00:04:55,659 --> 00:04:59,065
bogie, and special shock absorbers called yaw

134
00:04:59,065 --> 00:04:59,671
dampers.

135
00:04:59,851 --> 00:05:02,875
High speed trains are designed and tested

136
00:05:02,875 --> 00:05:05,457
to stay stable well above their top

137
00:05:05,457 --> 00:05:05,900
speed.

138
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:08,814
The same cone that steers the train

139
00:05:08,814 --> 00:05:10,299
can shake it apart,

140
00:05:10,299 --> 00:05:13,345
so the engineering is all about keeping

141
00:05:13,345 --> 00:05:14,439
the cone calm.

142
00:05:14,789 --> 00:05:18,260
Why are railway rails tilted slightly inward?

143
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:20,653
Look closely and you will see that

144
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:23,061
the rails are not perfectly vertical.

145
00:05:23,241 --> 00:05:25,832
They lean toward each other at a

146
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:28,666
small angle, often one in twenty or

147
00:05:28,666 --> 00:05:29,719
one in forty.

148
00:05:29,899 --> 00:05:31,977
The reason is the cone.

149
00:05:32,157 --> 00:05:34,901
A conical wheel touches the rail at

150
00:05:34,901 --> 00:05:37,331
an angle, so the rail is tilted

151
00:05:37,331 --> 00:05:38,272
to match it,

152
00:05:38,272 --> 00:05:41,015
and the contact point sits near the

153
00:05:41,015 --> 00:05:42,896
middle of the rail head.

154
00:05:43,247 --> 00:05:45,801
That small tilt matters a lot.

155
00:05:45,981 --> 00:05:48,809
It keeps the wheel force pushing down

156
00:05:48,809 --> 00:05:51,178
through the center of the rail,

157
00:05:51,178 --> 00:05:52,936
instead of on its edge,

158
00:05:52,936 --> 00:05:55,535
which would bend and wear the rail

159
00:05:55,535 --> 00:05:56,070
faster.

160
00:05:56,250 --> 00:05:58,872
The tilt is usually created by the

161
00:05:58,872 --> 00:06:01,340
steel plates or the shape of the

162
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:03,808
concrete sleeper under the rail.

163
00:06:03,988 --> 00:06:06,600
A tiny angle you can barely see,

164
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:09,212
built into millions of sleepers.

165
00:06:09,563 --> 00:06:12,486
The contact between a wheel and a

166
00:06:12,486 --> 00:06:14,700
rail is incredibly small,

167
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:17,446
about the size of a small coin.

168
00:06:17,626 --> 00:06:19,809
All the weight of that wheel,

169
00:06:19,809 --> 00:06:21,691
often more than ten tons,

170
00:06:21,691 --> 00:06:24,251
sits on that little patch of steel

171
00:06:24,251 --> 00:06:24,928
on steel.

172
00:06:25,108 --> 00:06:28,038
That is why trains roll so easily,

173
00:06:28,038 --> 00:06:30,019
with very low friction.

174
00:06:30,199 --> 00:06:32,326
And it is also why the shape

175
00:06:32,326 --> 00:06:34,832
of both surfaces must be perfect.

176
00:06:35,182 --> 00:06:37,659
Why do train cars sit on separate

177
00:06:37,659 --> 00:06:39,535
wheel frames that swivel?

178
00:06:39,715 --> 00:06:41,421
They are called bogies,

179
00:06:41,421 --> 00:06:42,978
or trucks in America.

180
00:06:43,158 --> 00:06:45,357
Each car has two of them,

181
00:06:45,357 --> 00:06:48,963
and each bogie usually has two wheelsets.

182
00:06:49,143 --> 00:06:52,292
Long cars cannot have wheels fixed directly

183
00:06:52,292 --> 00:06:53,171
to the body,

184
00:06:53,171 --> 00:06:54,563
because in a curve,

185
00:06:54,563 --> 00:06:56,980
the wheels would be forced in the

186
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:58,152
wrong direction.

187
00:06:58,332 --> 00:07:00,442
The bogie pivots under the car,

188
00:07:00,442 --> 00:07:02,756
so the wheels can follow the curve

189
00:07:02,756 --> 00:07:05,410
while the long body sweeps around above

190
00:07:05,410 --> 00:07:05,751
them.

191
00:07:06,101 --> 00:07:08,200
The bogie was a big step in

192
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:09,444
railway history.

193
00:07:09,624 --> 00:07:11,532
In the eighteen thirties,

194
00:07:11,532 --> 00:07:13,746
the American engineer John B.

195
00:07:13,926 --> 00:07:16,840
Jervis used a swiveling front truck on

196
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:19,601
a locomotive, so it could handle the

197
00:07:19,601 --> 00:07:22,668
sharp curves of early American railways.

198
00:07:22,848 --> 00:07:26,224
Today, the bogie also holds the springs,

199
00:07:26,224 --> 00:07:28,502
the dampers and the brakes.

200
00:07:28,682 --> 00:07:31,011
It is the part that decides how

201
00:07:31,011 --> 00:07:32,513
smooth your ride is.

202
00:07:32,864 --> 00:07:36,950
Some trains share bogies between two cars,

203
00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:38,994
called Jacobs bogies,

204
00:07:38,994 --> 00:07:41,037
used on France's TGV.

205
00:07:41,217 --> 00:07:44,544
This keeps the cars connected more rigidly,

206
00:07:44,544 --> 00:07:47,251
and in some accidents it has helped

207
00:07:47,251 --> 00:07:49,959
the train stay upright and in line.

208
00:07:50,139 --> 00:07:53,447
One clever placement of wheels can change

209
00:07:53,447 --> 00:07:55,867
how a whole train behaves in a

210
00:07:55,867 --> 00:07:56,351
crash.

211
00:07:56,701 --> 00:07:59,388
Why don't modern trains make the classic

212
00:07:59,388 --> 00:08:00,799
clickety-clack sound?

213
00:08:00,979 --> 00:08:03,661
Because the gaps in the rails are

214
00:08:03,661 --> 00:08:04,067
gone.

215
00:08:04,247 --> 00:08:07,555
Old railways used short rails joined with

216
00:08:07,555 --> 00:08:09,007
gaps between them,

217
00:08:09,007 --> 00:08:11,831
so the steel could expand in summer

218
00:08:11,831 --> 00:08:12,235
heat.

219
00:08:12,415 --> 00:08:14,399
Every gap made a click under each

220
00:08:14,399 --> 00:08:14,760
wheel.

221
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:19,061
Today, most main lines use continuous welded

222
00:08:19,061 --> 00:08:22,995
rail, with rails welded into long strings,

223
00:08:22,995 --> 00:08:27,022
often hundreds of meters or even kilometers

224
00:08:27,022 --> 00:08:27,490
long.

225
00:08:27,841 --> 00:08:30,453
But steel still expands in the heat.

226
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:33,231
So how can a rail kilometers long

227
00:08:33,231 --> 00:08:34,255
have no gaps?

228
00:08:34,435 --> 00:08:36,432
The trick is to stop it from

229
00:08:36,432 --> 00:08:36,931
moving.

230
00:08:37,111 --> 00:08:39,928
The rail is clamped tightly to heavy

231
00:08:39,928 --> 00:08:41,962
sleepers buried in stones,

232
00:08:41,962 --> 00:08:44,622
and it is installed at a carefully

233
00:08:44,622 --> 00:08:46,109
chosen temperature.

234
00:08:46,289 --> 00:08:47,486
In the cold,

235
00:08:47,486 --> 00:08:49,482
it pulls in tension.

236
00:08:49,662 --> 00:08:50,648
In the heat,

237
00:08:50,648 --> 00:08:52,704
it pushes in compression.

238
00:08:52,884 --> 00:08:54,152
It wants to grow,

239
00:08:54,152 --> 00:08:56,018
but it is not allowed to.

240
00:08:56,369 --> 00:08:58,682
If the heat is too strong,

241
00:08:58,682 --> 00:09:00,550
or the track is weak,

242
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:03,752
the rail can suddenly bend sideways.

243
00:09:03,932 --> 00:09:06,219
This is called a sun kink or

244
00:09:06,219 --> 00:09:08,994
buckle, and it can derail a train.

245
00:09:09,174 --> 00:09:12,238
That is why railways often slow trains

246
00:09:12,238 --> 00:09:14,012
down on very hot days,

247
00:09:14,012 --> 00:09:17,156
and why you sometimes see rails painted

248
00:09:17,156 --> 00:09:19,333
white, to reflect sunlight.

249
00:09:19,513 --> 00:09:22,090
A silent rail is a rail under

250
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:24,134
enormous hidden stress.

251
00:09:24,484 --> 00:09:26,875
Why do locomotives carry sand?

252
00:09:27,055 --> 00:09:30,609
Because steel wheels on steel rails have

253
00:09:30,609 --> 00:09:31,409
low grip.

254
00:09:31,589 --> 00:09:34,149
That is great for rolling easily,

255
00:09:34,149 --> 00:09:36,863
but bad when starting a heavy train

256
00:09:36,863 --> 00:09:38,880
or stopping on a wet rail.

257
00:09:39,060 --> 00:09:42,628
So locomotives and many trains carry sand

258
00:09:42,628 --> 00:09:45,499
in boxes, with pipes that blow it

259
00:09:45,499 --> 00:09:48,023
right in front of the wheels.

260
00:09:48,203 --> 00:09:51,329
The sand increases grip between the wheel

261
00:09:51,329 --> 00:09:53,922
and the rail at exactly the moment

262
00:09:53,922 --> 00:09:54,913
it is needed.

263
00:09:55,264 --> 00:09:58,828
In autumn, railways have an unexpected enemy.

264
00:09:59,008 --> 00:09:59,762
Leaves.

265
00:09:59,942 --> 00:10:02,480
When trains crush wet leaves on the

266
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:05,308
rails, they form a slippery black layer

267
00:10:05,308 --> 00:10:07,338
that can be compared to ice.

268
00:10:07,518 --> 00:10:10,798
Trains slip when starting and slide when

269
00:10:10,798 --> 00:10:11,454
braking.

270
00:10:11,634 --> 00:10:14,961
In Britain, special trains spray the rails

271
00:10:14,961 --> 00:10:17,813
with high pressure water and apply a

272
00:10:17,813 --> 00:10:19,397
gel mixed with sand,

273
00:10:19,397 --> 00:10:22,408
to clean the rails during leaf season.

274
00:10:22,758 --> 00:10:26,540
Modern trains are also controlled by computers

275
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:29,665
that detect wheelslip and wheel slide,

276
00:10:29,665 --> 00:10:32,543
a bit like the anti-lock brakes and

277
00:10:32,543 --> 00:10:34,681
traction control on a car.

278
00:10:34,861 --> 00:10:37,212
If a wheel starts spinning,

279
00:10:37,212 --> 00:10:39,041
the power is reduced.

280
00:10:39,221 --> 00:10:40,727
If it starts sliding,

281
00:10:40,727 --> 00:10:43,238
the brake is released for a moment.

282
00:10:43,418 --> 00:10:44,989
Sand plus software,

283
00:10:44,989 --> 00:10:48,050
the old and the new working together.

284
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:51,230
Why did railway workers walk along trains

285
00:10:51,230 --> 00:10:53,438
hitting the wheels with hammers?

286
00:10:53,618 --> 00:10:55,261
They were called wheel tappers,

287
00:10:55,261 --> 00:10:56,532
and they were listening.

288
00:10:56,712 --> 00:10:59,810
A healthy steel wheel rings like a

289
00:10:59,810 --> 00:11:00,265
bell.

290
00:11:00,445 --> 00:11:02,941
A cracked wheel makes a dull sound.

291
00:11:03,121 --> 00:11:04,643
It was a simple test,

292
00:11:04,643 --> 00:11:07,396
but it could find hidden cracks before

293
00:11:07,396 --> 00:11:09,425
they turned into a disaster.

294
00:11:09,605 --> 00:11:13,911
Today, railways use ultrasonic testing and sensors

295
00:11:13,911 --> 00:11:16,494
along the track to do the same

296
00:11:16,494 --> 00:11:16,838
job.

297
00:11:17,189 --> 00:11:19,197
The reason is fatigue.

298
00:11:19,377 --> 00:11:21,817
A wheel takes millions of loads,

299
00:11:21,817 --> 00:11:23,876
and a tiny crack can grow a

300
00:11:23,876 --> 00:11:25,554
little with each turn.

301
00:11:25,734 --> 00:11:26,838
On June third,

302
00:11:26,838 --> 00:11:30,466
nineteen ninety-eight, a German high speed ICE

303
00:11:30,466 --> 00:11:33,384
train near the village of Eschede had

304
00:11:33,384 --> 00:11:35,435
a wheel with a steel tire,

305
00:11:35,435 --> 00:11:37,880
a ring around the wheel center,

306
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:39,851
that had a fatigue crack.

307
00:11:40,031 --> 00:11:42,612
The tire broke at about two hundred

308
00:11:42,612 --> 00:11:44,014
kilometers an hour.

309
00:11:44,194 --> 00:11:46,996
The train derailed and hit a bridge,

310
00:11:46,996 --> 00:11:49,488
and one hundred one people died.

311
00:11:49,838 --> 00:11:53,417
After Eschede, those wheels were replaced with

312
00:11:53,417 --> 00:11:55,207
solid one-piece wheels,

313
00:11:55,207 --> 00:11:58,475
and inspection rules became much stricter.

314
00:11:58,655 --> 00:12:01,829
It remains the deadliest high speed rail

315
00:12:01,829 --> 00:12:03,415
accident in Germany.

316
00:12:03,595 --> 00:12:06,001
And it shows why the old hammer

317
00:12:06,001 --> 00:12:08,483
test existed in the first place.

318
00:12:08,663 --> 00:12:09,557
On a train,

319
00:12:09,557 --> 00:12:12,402
the most dangerous crack is the one

320
00:12:12,402 --> 00:12:13,621
you cannot see.

321
00:12:13,971 --> 00:12:17,063
Why are many train couplers shaped like

322
00:12:17,063 --> 00:12:18,173
a curled hand?

323
00:12:18,353 --> 00:12:20,217
It is a knuckle coupler,

324
00:12:20,217 --> 00:12:22,858
and it was invented to save lives.

325
00:12:23,038 --> 00:12:26,066
Before it, many railways used link and

326
00:12:26,066 --> 00:12:27,101
pin couplers.

327
00:12:27,281 --> 00:12:30,024
A worker had to stand between two

328
00:12:30,024 --> 00:12:32,600
moving cars, hold a metal link,

329
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:34,595
and drop a pin in at the

330
00:12:34,595 --> 00:12:35,676
right moment.

331
00:12:35,856 --> 00:12:38,746
Thousands of workers lost fingers,

332
00:12:38,746 --> 00:12:41,382
hands, or their lives doing it.

333
00:12:41,732 --> 00:12:43,689
In eighteen seventy-three,

334
00:12:43,689 --> 00:12:46,701
Eli Janney patented the knuckle coupler.

335
00:12:46,881 --> 00:12:49,285
When two cars are pushed together,

336
00:12:49,285 --> 00:12:52,254
the knuckles close and lock automatically,

337
00:12:52,254 --> 00:12:54,729
like two hands gripping each other.

338
00:12:54,909 --> 00:12:56,188
To separate them,

339
00:12:56,188 --> 00:12:58,519
a worker pulls a lever from the

340
00:12:58,519 --> 00:12:59,723
side of the car,

341
00:12:59,723 --> 00:13:01,979
without stepping between them.

342
00:13:02,159 --> 00:13:04,163
In eighteen ninety-three,

343
00:13:04,163 --> 00:13:07,769
the United States passed the Safety Appliance

344
00:13:07,769 --> 00:13:11,455
Act, which required automatic couplers and air

345
00:13:11,455 --> 00:13:12,817
brakes on trains.

346
00:13:13,168 --> 00:13:16,508
Variations of Janney's design are still used

347
00:13:16,508 --> 00:13:19,620
across North America and on many railways

348
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:20,911
around the world,

349
00:13:20,911 --> 00:13:23,796
and its basic shape has barely changed

350
00:13:23,796 --> 00:13:25,846
in one hundred fifty years.

351
00:13:26,026 --> 00:13:28,613
A coupler today can hold together a

352
00:13:28,613 --> 00:13:31,645
freight train weighing thousands of tons.

353
00:13:31,825 --> 00:13:34,362
It is one of the most successful

354
00:13:34,362 --> 00:13:36,662
safety inventions in history,

355
00:13:36,662 --> 00:13:39,278
and almost nobody knows its name.

356
00:13:39,629 --> 00:13:42,302
Why do the hoses between train cars

357
00:13:42,302 --> 00:13:43,448
matter so much?

358
00:13:43,628 --> 00:13:46,229
They carry compressed air for the brakes.

359
00:13:46,409 --> 00:13:49,262
In the eighteen sixties and seventies,

360
00:13:49,262 --> 00:13:53,166
George Westinghouse developed the railway air brake.

361
00:13:53,346 --> 00:13:55,633
And his key idea was brilliant.

362
00:13:55,813 --> 00:13:57,836
In the automatic air brake,

363
00:13:57,836 --> 00:14:00,384
air pressure in the pipe keeps the

364
00:14:00,384 --> 00:14:01,583
brakes released.

365
00:14:01,763 --> 00:14:04,782
To brake, the driver lowers the pressure.

366
00:14:04,962 --> 00:14:06,773
So if the pipe breaks,

367
00:14:06,773 --> 00:14:08,996
or the train splits in two,

368
00:14:08,996 --> 00:14:12,206
the pressure drops and every car brakes

369
00:14:12,206 --> 00:14:13,112
on its own.

370
00:14:13,462 --> 00:14:16,179
This is called fail-safe design.

371
00:14:16,359 --> 00:14:18,855
The system is built so that its

372
00:14:18,855 --> 00:14:21,834
most likely failure leads to the safe

373
00:14:21,834 --> 00:14:23,847
outcome, a stopped train.

374
00:14:24,027 --> 00:14:25,473
Before air brakes,

375
00:14:25,473 --> 00:14:28,606
workers called brakemen had to climb on

376
00:14:28,606 --> 00:14:31,177
top of moving cars and turn hand

377
00:14:31,177 --> 00:14:33,186
wheels to brake each car,

378
00:14:33,186 --> 00:14:35,033
often in rain and snow.

379
00:14:35,213 --> 00:14:37,129
Many fell to their deaths.

380
00:14:37,309 --> 00:14:39,936
The air brake made trains longer,

381
00:14:39,936 --> 00:14:41,767
faster, and much safer.

382
00:14:42,117 --> 00:14:45,762
The same idea is everywhere in railways.

383
00:14:45,942 --> 00:14:49,065
A driver must keep pressing a pedal

384
00:14:49,065 --> 00:14:52,455
or button, called a dead man's switch,

385
00:14:52,455 --> 00:14:54,151
or the train stops.

386
00:14:54,331 --> 00:14:57,326
Signals turn red when they lose power.

387
00:14:57,506 --> 00:14:58,491
On a railway,

388
00:14:58,491 --> 00:14:59,930
if something fails,

389
00:14:59,930 --> 00:15:02,278
it should fail toward stopping.

390
00:15:02,458 --> 00:15:05,323
A train that stops by mistake is

391
00:15:05,323 --> 00:15:06,486
an annoyance.

392
00:15:06,666 --> 00:15:08,958
A train that cannot stop is a

393
00:15:08,958 --> 00:15:09,906
catastrophe.

394
00:15:10,256 --> 00:15:11,719
So here are the ten.

395
00:15:11,899 --> 00:15:14,845
A cone that steers without a steering

396
00:15:14,845 --> 00:15:17,393
wheel, and a flange that is only

397
00:15:17,393 --> 00:15:18,110
a backup.

398
00:15:18,290 --> 00:15:21,604
A solid axle that needs no differential,

399
00:15:21,604 --> 00:15:24,090
and rails that lean in to meet

400
00:15:24,090 --> 00:15:24,339
it.

401
00:15:24,519 --> 00:15:26,055
A swiveling bogie,

402
00:15:26,055 --> 00:15:27,676
rails without gaps,

403
00:15:27,676 --> 00:15:28,870
sand for grip,

404
00:15:28,870 --> 00:15:31,771
a hammer that listened for cracks,

405
00:15:31,771 --> 00:15:34,245
a coupler shaped like a hand,

406
00:15:34,245 --> 00:15:37,060
and brakes that work when the air

407
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:37,743
is lost.

408
00:15:38,093 --> 00:15:40,303
The next time you ride a train,

409
00:15:40,303 --> 00:15:43,155
remember that it is steering itself with

410
00:15:43,155 --> 00:15:43,654
a cone.

411
00:15:43,834 --> 00:15:46,841
If you want more machines explained part

412
00:15:46,841 --> 00:15:49,697
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

413
00:15:49,877 --> 00:15:51,917
And tell us in the comments which

414
00:15:51,917 --> 00:15:53,957
machine you want us to take apart

415
00:15:53,957 --> 00:15:54,266
next.
